  "prompt_text": "The figure presents low-valent early transition metal complexes as highly reactive species made from earth-abundant metals. At the center is a large circle representing this class of complexes, surrounded by example metals and oxidation states, including Sc, Ti, Y, Zr, Nb, Hf, and Ta in low oxidation states such as II or III. Small lightning-bolt symbols with “e−” above the circle emphasize electron-rich or reduced character, suggesting these compounds are generated or behave through added electrons.\n\nThe main message is that these low-valent complexes enable a broad range of synthetic and catalytic reactivity. On one side, the figure highlights more traditional bond-forming and bond-reorganization chemistry, such as reductive coupling, group-transfer catalysis, and (de)hydrofunctionalization. On the other side, it emphasizes reactions involving electron flow, including single-electron transfer, proton-coupled electron transfer, and photoredox catalysis.\n\nOverall, the visual links a family of early transition metals to two major reactivity modes: classical organometallic transformations and newer electron-transfer-based processes. The comparison suggests that reduced complexes of abundant metals can serve as versatile platforms for both catalytic bond construction and redox chemistry.",
